---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('local-scholarships-better-odds');

const faqJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  'mainEntity': [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Are local scholarships easier to win than national ones?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'Usually, because each one is open to a much smaller group. With made-up numbers: a national award paying $10,000 to one of 10,000 applicants is worth about a dollar per application, and a local award paying $1,000 to one of 25 is worth about forty. The local one also takes less time, and the same essay and reference letter usually work for several of them.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Where do I find local scholarships in Alberta?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'The usual sources are your guidance office (some awards are distributed only through schools), city and county programs, service clubs such as Rotary, Lions, Elks and Legion branches, community foundations that administer many small named funds, and employers and unions, both yours and your parents\'.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'What do local scholarship committees look for?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'They know your context, including your school and often your referee, and each award draws from a much smaller group than a provincial or national one. They also know what a part-time job, farm work or coaching takes in your town.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Should I skip national scholarships and only apply locally?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'No. Apply to national awards where you are competitive, but do not treat small local awards as the consolation round. Small awards stack, and $1,000 three times is a semester\'s tuition.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      'name': 'Do all community foundations fund student scholarships?',
      'acceptedAnswer': {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        'text': 'No. Plenty of them, including the Community Foundation of Southeastern Alberta, give only to charities rather than to students. Check that the foundation near you funds students before applying.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqJson)} slot="ld" />

  <p>
    A national scholarship is open to every Grade 12 student in Canada. A $1,000 award from a Medicine Hat service club is open to the graduates of a few schools. If the goal is money for school, apply to the second kind as seriously as the first.
  </p>

  <h2>The arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    To show the shape with made-up numbers: suppose a national award pays $10,000 and draws 10,000 applicants, while a local award pays $1,000 and draws 25. The expected value of the national application is a dollar; the local one is forty. The local award also takes less time to apply for, and the same essay and reference letter usually work for several of them. Applying to five local awards is a few evenings of work with good odds; a national award is a long shot that costs a month.
  </p>
  <p>
    Apply to the big ones too if you are a real candidate for them, and do not treat the small ones as the consolation round.
  </p>

  <h2>What local committees look at</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>They know your context.</strong> A national reader does not know your school or what it means to hold a job in a small town. A local committee does, and often knows your referee personally.</li>
    <li><strong>They draw from fewer people.</strong> An award open to one town's graduates competes for a much smaller group than a provincial or national one, so a complete application on time already counts.</li>
    <li><strong>They value ordinary evidence.</strong> Local committees know what a part-time job, farm work, coaching or volunteering takes in your town.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where local awards hide</h2>
  <p>
    Local awards are hard to find. They live on photocopied sheets in guidance offices and outdated municipal webpages, which is the problem <a href="/about/">ScholarAB exists to fix</a>. The usual sources:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your guidance office.</strong> Ask for the local awards list directly; some awards are only distributed through schools.</li>
    <li><strong>City and county programs</strong>, like <a href="/scholarships/cypress-county-agricultural-service-board-bursary/">Cypress County's two Agricultural Service Board bursaries</a> or the <a href="/scholarships/lethbridge-county-community-benefit-scholarship/">Lethbridge County Community Benefit Scholarship</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Service clubs</strong>: Rotary, Lions, Elks, Legion branches and their auxiliaries. The <a href="/scholarships/kin-canada-bursary/">Kin Canada Bursary</a>, endorsed by your nearest Kin club, is typical of the shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Community foundations</strong> that administer dozens of small named funds through a single application. Check that the one near you funds students, though: plenty of them, including the Community Foundation of Southeastern Alberta, give only to charities.</li>
    <li><strong>Employers and unions</strong>, both yours and your parents'. Co-op and credit-union memberships often carry student awards; <a href="/scholarships/south-country-co-op-scholarship/">South Country Co-op</a> and the <a href="/scholarships/ata-local-21-scholarship/">ATA Local 21 Scholarship</a> are two you can look at right now.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In <a href="/scholarships/">the directory</a>, listings are tagged by region, and the SCOPE row has a chip for each one: <a href="/scholarships/medicine-hat/">Medicine Hat</a>, <a href="/scholarships/calgary/">Calgary</a>, <a href="/scholarships/edmonton/">Edmonton</a>, <a href="/scholarships/lethbridge/">Lethbridge</a>, <a href="/scholarships/red-deer/">Red Deer</a> and Alberta-wide. Each city also has its own page, linked here, so you can see your whole local pool without touching a filter, and <a href="/match/">Build my combo</a> sorts it by how well it fits your answers.
  </p>

  <h2>What to apply to</h2>
  <p>
    Apply to the <a href="/guides/alexander-rutherford-scholarship-guide/">Rutherford</a> because it is guaranteed money for marks you already have. Apply to every local award you plausibly qualify for, reusing one strong <a href="/guides/how-to-write-a-scholarship-essay/">essay</a> and one <a href="/guides/reference-letters-for-scholarships/">reference package</a>. Add national awards on top only where you are competitive. Small awards stack, and $1,000 three times is a semester's tuition.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
